				<h2>NSW Supreme Court Probate Application Requirements, Timeframes, and Costs </h2>
            </div>
            <div class="probate-intro">
        	<p class="probate__lead">    
				<p>The probate process NSW requires specific documents and fees to be paid and timeframes 
                set by the Supreme Court probate registry. Understanding requirements early helps executors 
                avoid delays from requisitions due to missing information or disputes, and ensures the probate 
                application progresses smoothly through the NSW probate system.</p>
                <br>
                <p> </p>
                   <p>
                   &nbsp;
                   </p>
			</div>
            
			<div class="instruments">
				<article class="instrument">
					<span class="instrument__num">01</span>
					<h3>Application Requirements</h3>
					<p>As a minimum an application for a Grant of Probate requires the application to be accompanied by the original will, a certified copy of the 
                    death certificate,  executor details, an inventory of property and the preparation of various Supreme Court probate forms such as affidavits from the executor. 
                    The executor must also publish a probate notice NSW when lodging documents with the probate registry of NSW to begin the process.</p>
                    </a>
				</article>
				<article class="instrument">
					<span class="instrument__num">02</span>
					<h3>Typical Timeframes</h3>
                    <h3>How long does it take to obtain a Grant Probate in NSW?</h3>
					<p>In NSW this usually depends on the court’s workload and whether documents are correct. 
                       A relatively straightforward NSW probate application commonly takes 3-4 weeks, although delays can occur if documents need to be obtained before 
                       the probate application is lodged or if the court issues requisitions requiring further documentation.</p>
					<!--- <a class="instrument__link" href="/legal-services/power-of-attorney">Commercial Property Sales</a> --->
				</article>
				<article class="instrument">
					<span class="instrument__num">03</span>
					<h3>Court Filing Fees</h3>
					<p>The Supreme Court of NSW charges a fee for all probate applications for estates with a value exceeding $100,000. 
                       The Supreme Court of NSW probate fee is determined by the gross value of the estate, with larger estates attracting higher filing fees, 
                       and executors must pay these fees when submitting the probate application, making it important to correctly calculate asset values and 
                       follow probate registry NSW filing instructions.</p>
					<!--- <a class="instrument__link" href="/legal-services/enduring-guardian">Retirement Village Sales</a> --->
				</article>
				<article class="instrument">
					<span class="instrument__num">04</span>
					<h3>Solicitor Fees</h3>
					<p>Where an executor engages a solicitor in NSW to assist with preparing and lodging a probate application, 
                       solicitor's fees are regulated by law and are calculated based on the gross value of the estate, with larger estates attracting higher fees.

                       The regulated fees can often be deferred until the executor collects sufficient estate assets 
                       (i.e. proceeds of bank accounts or the sale of real estate) to meet the solicitor’s fees. 

                       The solicitor may also charge fees leading up to or after the probate application. 
                       These fees are not regulated and are usually charged on an hourly basis.</p>
					<!---  <a class="instrument__link" href="/legal-services/enduring-guardian">Vacant Land Sales</a> --->
				</article>
				<article class="instrument">
					<span class="instrument__num">05</span>
					<h3>Requisitions and Delays</h3>
					<p>Requisitions may occur for many reasons including when the probate registry requests additional documents.

                       Delays may occur where the application is incomplete, missing documents or where there are doubts 
                       regarding due execution. An executor may benefit from engaging a probate lawyer to ensure documents 
                       meet requirements and reduce the risk of requisitions.</p>
